.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt MREMAP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mremap
.Nd resize or move a memory mapping
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/mman.h
.Ft "void *"
.Fn mremap "void *old_address" "size_t old_size" "size_t new_size" "int flags" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn mremap
system call changes the size or the address of the mapping of the range
that starts at
.Fa old_address
and is
.Fa old_size
bytes long, or maps the pages of a shared mapping a second time.
The
.Fa old_address
argument must be a multiple of the page size.
The kernel rounds
.Fa old_size
and
.Fa new_size
up to a multiple of the page size.
.Pp
Without flags, a smaller
.Fa new_size
removes the end of the range as
.Xr munmap 2
does, and a larger
.Fa new_size
extends the mapping in place.
If the addresses after the range are not free, the call fails.
.Pp
The range must be mapped completely, by one mapping or by adjacent
mappings of one kind: the same protection, maximum protection,
inheritance and wiring, and, for a file or a shared memory object,
consecutive parts of the same object.
The new part of a larger mapping continues the last mapping of the range:
new anonymous memory for anonymous memory, and the next part of the file
or the shared memory object for a mapping of one.
A reference to a page past the end of the file or the object raises
.Dv SIGBUS .
If the range is wired with
.Xr mlock 2 ,
the kernel wires the new part too.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument is 0 or the bitwise OR of these values:
.Bl -tag -width MREMAP_DONTUNMAP
.It Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE
If the mapping cannot grow in place, move it to an address that the kernel
selects as for
.Xr mmap 2 .
.It Dv MREMAP_FIXED
Move the mapping to the address in the fifth argument,
.Fa "void *new_address" .
This address must be a multiple of the page size, and the new range must
not overlap the old range.
Mappings in the new range are removed first, as for
.Xr mmap 2
with
.Dv MAP_FIXED .
This flag needs
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE .
Without it, the kernel ignores the fifth argument.
.It Dv MREMAP_DONTUNMAP
Move the mapping, and map the old range again with the same protection:
new anonymous memory, filled with zeros, in place of private anonymous
memory, and the same pages in place of a shared mapping.
This flag needs
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE ,
and
.Fa new_size
must be equal to
.Fa old_size .
.El
.Pp
A move keeps the pages, the protection, the maximum protection, the
inheritance and the wiring of the mapping.
No data is copied.
.Pp
If
.Fa old_size
is 0 and
.Fa old_address
is in a shared mapping, the call makes a second mapping of
.Fa new_size
bytes of the same object, from the offset that
.Fa old_address
maps.
The old mapping does not change.
This needs
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE .
.Pp
The call does not change executable mappings, stacks that grow down and
their guard pages, guards made with
.Dv MAP_GUARD ,
device mappings, or System V shared memory.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Upon successful completion,
.Fn mremap
returns the address of the mapping.
Otherwise the value
.Dv MAP_FAILED
is returned and
.Va errno
is set to indicate the error.
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn mremap
system call
will fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa flags
argument has an unknown bit, or
.Dv MREMAP_FIXED
or
.Dv MREMAP_DONTUNMAP
without
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa old_address
argument is not a multiple of the page size, or
.Fa new_size
is 0.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
With
.Dv MREMAP_FIXED ,
.Fa new_address
is not a multiple of the page size, the new range is outside the address
space of the process, or it overlaps the old range.
With an
.Fa old_size
of 0, the old range is the page at
.Fa old_address .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Dv MREMAP_DONTUNMAP
with a change of size or for a private file mapping.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An
.Fa old_size
of 0 without
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE ,
or for a mapping that is not shared.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The range has a mapping that the call does not change, such as an
executable mapping.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
For a mapping of large pages from
.Xr shm_create_largepage 3 ,
an address or a size is not a multiple of the large page size.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A larger private file mapping after a write to it, or an offset in the
object past
.Dv OFF_MAX .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The range is not mapped completely, or its mappings are of different
kinds.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The mapping cannot grow in place and
.Dv MREMAP_MAYMOVE
is not set, or no address range is free for a move.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The new size is too large, or the call would exceed the
.Dv RLIMIT_VMEM
resource limit or the swap space limits.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The range is wired and the new part would exceed the
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
resource limit or the system limit of wired pages.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr madvise 2 ,
.Xr minherit 2 ,
.Xr mlock 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr mprotect 2 ,
.Xr munmap 2 ,
.Xr shm_open 2
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Fn mremap
system call is a
.Fx
extension that is compatible with the Linux system call of the same name.
The
.Nx
.Fn mremap
has another order of arguments.
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn mremap
system call first appeared in the freebsd-android-native fork of
.Fx 15.1 .
.Sh BUGS
The call does not move executable mappings.
A private file mapping that has a private copy of a page cannot grow.
A moved page is mapped again at its first reference, not at the move.
The
.Dv PROT_BTI
state of the old range does not move with the mapping.
If the kernel cannot wire a page of the new part of a wired mapping, the
call succeeds and the new part is not wired.
If a move with
.Dv MREMAP_FIXED
fails for lack of swap space, the mappings that were in the new range can
be removed already.
